AME Region Anti-illicit Trade Project

Project Initiation Document Date: 23 March 2026

Project Initiation Document

Background

Africa Middle East Region is heavily infected region by illicit trade. Overall regional best estimate for illicit trade
is 74 billion sticks or 13.8% share (source: Phoenix) ranging from less than 1% in Egypt and Armenia to as high
as 50% in Georgia. However, we need to handle this figure with caution as reliable quantitative research for
illicit trade only exists in South Africa. Therefore a margin of error can be significant elsewhere due to the

nature of this activity.

The prime reasons behind the existence of this level of illicit trade are the porous borders of Africa, generally
accepted culture of corruption in many places, and low levels of enforcement and penalties as well as a

geographical proximity of very poor markets surrounding high value markets.
The region is also a key manufacturer of products that end up as illicit partly for consumption within the region
but also for the rest of the world. The manufacturing is featured by the 7 cigarette factories operating in the UAE

duty free zones and compounded by small independent factories in the Southern part of Africa.

BAT as a main player in Africa is estimated to lose 14bn sticks per annum or £56 million profit from illicit traders.

This loss is despite the excellent enforcement work of some of our key markets like South Africa and Nigeria.

Once established illicit trade is very difficult to remove and should be addressed as soon as possible with a

coordinated regional approach to assure that the issue is addressed effectively.

