AME Region Anti-illicit Trade Project

Project Initiation Document Date: 23 March 2026

Project Definition (Incorporating Business Case)
Overall Project Objectives
The overall objectives of the project are to:

e The aspiration of the project is to create an opportunity to reduce illicit trade incidence by an overall 0.77
percentage points or 4.1bn sticks in the region (or 2.9pp in the affected areas) and as a result to create an

opportunity for BAT to sell 1.5bn sticks more by mid 2008 '. This will be achieved by:
o Project Jessie - BATSA and protection belt countries

Applying a pro-active, multi-disciplined, co-ordinated and business-integrated approach, aligned
with the global BAT six pillar methodology to protect BATSA’s volume base and brand equity by
driving down illicit trade, and maintaining operational sustainability. The aim is to decrease illicit
trade by 2% points. Illicit trade was measured as 19.3% average in 2006, which accounted for

5.7bn sticks. A 2% point reduction will therefore account for 600m sticks.
o Project Dawn — BAT Nigeria

By applying a comprehensive 6 pillar AIT strategy’, significantly enhance BATN’s ability to
effectively combat the incidence of illicit trade in the Nigerian market. The aim is to increase BAT’s
volume, reduce illicit and unfair trade volume and level the playing field in Nigeria Area market. To

attain a reduction of illicit trade by 620 million sticks by Q2 2008 in contribution to Regional target.

o Project Ebony — UAE Manufacturing and transit hub

World class, systematic intelligence gathering of illicit tobacco trading and manufacturing activities

in order to provide actionable information for BAT end markets.

I The 1.5bn was chosen as an aspirational objective by the project team with the view of having an imperfect measure is
better than having no measure. The project team is in full awareness of the limitation on quantifying the current illicit trade
base and the challenge to proportionate any volume gains just as a pure effect of successfully fighting illicit products.

2 Traditionally some BAT markets approached AIT as purely a Brand Enforcement operation. The project aims to ensure
that priority markets approach the fight against illicit trade using the BAT six pillar methodology, involving all company
resources necessary. A comprehensive six pillar AIT / Excise strategy as referred to in this document encompasses the use
of company mobilisation, service providers, Government relations, political support, media, and intelligence. It recognises
the close links between brand portfolio, excise and illicit trade. The project also aims to ensure that priority markets address
resource issues to ensure that AIT receives adequate attention in line with the value of the market and size of the problem.
In line with Phoenix recommendations, Finance Directors will carry responsibility for the workstreams. The only exception is
BATSA, where AIT is the responsibility of the Legal Director.

Ref.: DOC-0000004804 Page 5/17
AME Region Anti-illicit Trade Project

Project Initiation Document Date: 23 March 2026

o Project Harmony - Mastermind

The objective of this project is to apply an above market concerted approach to obstruct
Mastermind’s illicit activity in Southern Africa through the closure of the Mastermind Rwanda
factories in Uganda and DRC either permanently or temporary due to the use of counterfeit
Ugandan & DRC tax stamps, lobbying for heavy fines levied due to tax evasion and resulting in the
de-registering of the Super match brand if proven illicit. The potential of Project Harmony is to

remove 2,4bn illicit sticks from the markets.

o Project Paige - MEC

By building a comprehensive 6 pillar AIT strategy, significantly enhance BAT’s ability to effectively
combat unfair competition in Iran and GCC markets. Proactive intelligence gathering relating to
those involved in the illicit tobacco trading in Iran and KSA in order to, through engagement with
authorities disrupt this illicit trade and provide market opportunities for BAT. The total benefit of the

workstream should provide for the removal of 500m-800m illicit sticks from the markets.

e Building sustainable, innovative intelligence capabilities to allow further reductions in illicit trade.

e To build and promote an AIT culture within AME with high profile ownership evidenced in proper resourcing,

regular visibility of progress or lack of progress in all Zones, Area top teams and the RLT.

The target of removing 4.1bn cigarettes from the market will not be achieved by confiscations alone. It can be

achieved in various ways:

1.

2.

Confiscation of counterfeit or smuggled product by authorities.

Closing down of illegal manufacturers. Benefit will be calculated loss of production for such illegal

facilities in the remaining period for the project.
Closing down and disruption of illicit supply chains.
Reduction in illicit incidence in affected markets.

Achievement of volume benefit for BAT markets as a direct result of applying an integrated 6 pillar

combined Excise / AIT strategy.

