Project Quality Plan

Acceptance Criteria

The project will be deemed to have been successful if the following criteria are met:

Achieving our overall project objectives.

Workstreams achieving workstream objectives.

Quality Expectations

Workstream plans will be reviewed by the project manager, project executive, central AIT and the

regional brand enforcement and investigation manager.
Meet RLT expectations as our contribution to the Phoenix Programme.
Meet Central AIT expectations for successful AIT management.
o Close cooperation with Central AIT.
Deliver project through use of Prince2
Delivery of the project workstreams to agreed timelines as per PID.
A mixture of formal and informal quality reviews will control workstream quality.

o Formal quality reviews will be used where product quality is critical to the success of the project
and will involve a quality review meeting to determine the success of workstreams. This

procedure will result in the formal sign-off/acceptance of the workstreams.

Project Controls

The project does not allow for much flexibility regards tolerance levels. The tolerance levels are listed below:

Time — Much of the execution of enforcement action is controlled by external Government agencies and
judicial authorities. We do not have control over their timelines. When a deviation of such timelines
becomes apparent, it will be communicated to the PM who will cascade upwards. Such deviations

should be expected in this environment. Time changes will be reviewed by the PM and PE
Cost — As per workstream budget.

Benefits — All effort will be concentrated on achieving the benefits.

Quality — 0% tolerance

Scope — 0% tolerance, unless otherwise recommended by project board / RLT

Risk — 0% tolerance (as soon as risk changes will be reported as will impact implementation)

